0c5859c1e93e9f46e326c94e" "q-112460/selection/6434096b15c030b32afe93a5640dee16c6842e1898343cd7ad452d2c2954486a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-112460" :question/id #uuid "e683ad88-dd0a-5bae-b8ce-ef0171100317" :question/math-academy-id "q-112460" :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -6 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-(2)(-6) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-5\\lambda +6 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -2)(\\lambda -3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=2$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=3.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(3)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-112460/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112460"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109338/selection/4e60a46958d1ed6c3fd1dc9d1e5567093c7ca247e7d389af17461e20684ea253" :answer/id #uuid "c4aacab1-bb98-5d84-b8f7-c087835f59cc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 8\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109338/selection/4eebab2f3b261b452fe5dc6257904d0416de47fbf0476b310a5ddc8f4c54732c" :answer/id #uuid "0f595ddf-9cdd-57d5-917f-d3af0b82e9f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 1