$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}8-\\lambda  & 6 \\\\ 4 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (8-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-(6)(4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-6\\lambda -40 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -10)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=10.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(-4)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(10)=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 10\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109338/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109338"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109316/selection/8655356eed72d7e6e28170ea8122b9d9dae8d2ce3b7d2c5e64c9abd7db3eca01" :answer/id #uuid "7bb4055d-9441-5b50-b47b-658c6e1ed646" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109316/selection/e5f8f0fe01fcb90c2adc82baf1b9d892154934cf5d4947bcab00d3000cd184c4" :answer/id #uuid "464cd544-baf2-5319-9ec1-e22e6ca3a66d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109316/selection/cdc5060b900c268b5fcf12abdad3992de5d4ef164bde373718d98ae4e33e7862" :answer/id #uuid "c2328658-c225-507b-a244-ce75bd6d9e7d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109316/selection/6434096b15c030b32afe93a5640dee16c6842e1898343cd7ad452d2c2954486a" :answer/id #uuid "9d258eff-47ea-5be6-b279-22df7fabbb76" :