da }_{1}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=2.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(-3)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-109316/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "65f20482-73ea-5f1c-bb2b-1d7472fbfd4e"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109316"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-108719"] :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 5 \\\\ -10 & -9-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (6-\\lambda )(-9-\\lambda )-(5)(-10) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+3\\lambda -4 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda +4) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}=1$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-4.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-4)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} [:db/retract 17592186176204 :question/answer-fields 17592186173886] {:db/id "q-112453/selection/ma-v1/715997a8e9f066615bfe5727c669faf0307e21c1bd33d26c8462f09a4e96fec3/11951e1f02463b3dd6915c80f4d8ef5c64dc493b58b4f754584a096883696d8d" :answer/id #uuid "328c39bf-617a-5ff0-b63a-0c53492c2db6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6\\end{aligned}]" :db/ens