ical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}10-\\lambda  & -1 \\\\ 1 & 8-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (10-\\lambda )(8-\\lambda )-(-1)\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-18\\lambda +81 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -9)(\\lambda -9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=9.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(9)=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 9\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-112478/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-112478"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-112477/selection/fec5f29f10bf24b48f3759811edc76a4c863fa0ed9c5b9389d0c6052d46ca987" :answer/id #uuid "88dd73ac-cc0c-5a2d-954b-cba7393aba77" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112477/selection/f92c7fd47e0293262fcb028bdcd97f7da9d0cefa2a28d20e50af84d7d1214754" :answer/id #uuid "e4665c0b-c5e3-542f-8d6d-3d4b7265314c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112477/selection/7e8173437c1e035583401b8a2bf643bceea094061f4ff54afe22dd3c2d1b8f8b" :answer/id #uuid "9198e673-7cd0-53e1-b79a-54f68a98d4dc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-112477/selection/e93a168901e1ff546ab13ba78724c428c5a9409cc7adc5e8d69736f6401c2be8" :answer/id #uuid "3e8199fe-cfa2-5a64-a0e8-32ee8e994e87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer