7f411d99b4bfe21" "q-109102/selection/9be124f3d1c0f9e898bb6915575a498db341da137fa127559098027ae6b98c9e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-109102" :question/id #uuid "07aab14d-6173-554d-a730-1e67dec81067" :question/math-academy-id "q-109102" :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].$" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA=PJ{P}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (3-\\lambda )(-1-\\lambda )-1\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)(\\lambda -1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109102/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109102"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-109315/selection/25227d99205ffc311a7cc7e6c55ee45ea3b0baebaa064e397b8f6ae27a1bb2c9" :answer/id #uuid "8e264fa1-db8c-526d-ab4d-9e1cbd9f16fb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-109315/selection/c331d66c36470cbdfea9d5263d8b5a844f564fd3212ced158c1ab0490f5db256" :answer/id #uuid "823e22ab-a812-5619-8226-046e482958cd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\