s a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ -2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-2\\cdot (-2) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +25 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -5)(\\lambda -5) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=5.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\nJ={J}_{2}(5)=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-109315/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b629bead-e64a-5e73-ae42-80d4ebb3762b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-109315"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9087"] :question/problem "Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/1a6c3918b93419f9576951374ebd69b2df271106345493084ffa0797b0a35847.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}5-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ 2 & -2-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (5-\\lambda )(-2-\\lambda )-4\\cdot 2 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-3\\lambda -18 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -6)(\\lambda +3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=-3$ and $\\lambda_2=6.$ Since we get two distinct eigenvalues, the matrix $A$ is diagonalizable, and a Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/d6c87739f52f450b27c68a118bba04e51e68a377da7d0a16ae0658e1b2d547f0.png)\n\n**Note**: A Jordan canonical form is unique up to permutations of its Jordan blocks. Therefore, another Jordan canonical form for this matrix is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/7bd509d943bf0707e75c9436aee3a511f8366502bbf8121b715cec8ce366953b.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9226"] :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/4b66afe6c1771afad4987215e3ddc02865a9f873ae93050f940c36bc3ff86356.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix. \n\nNotice that $A$ is a scalar matrix, which means that it is already in Jordan canonical form. Therefore, it has a single (repeated) eigenvalue $\\lambda = -3,$ and\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/2be33154f6a1933a12a3c43474464edb9c4e3fd2b2a4213d789b49fc1e4aec77.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-9225"] :question/problem "Find the Jordan canonical form of the matrix \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/69fcceae9cb181cf79e1b12f0db37ed2ba27841aaebb089ffa24d0d365b9db56.png)" :question/worked-solution "Every $n\\times n$ matrix $A$ can be written in the form\n\n$$\nA = PJP^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere $J$ is a Jordan canonical form (or Jordan normal form) of $A,$ and $P$ is an invertible matrix.\n\nTo find the matrix $J,$ we first find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}2-\\lambda  & 3 \\\\ -3 & -4-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (2-\\lambda )(-4-\\lambda )-3\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda +1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1.$ \n\nSince $A$ is not a scalar matrix, we conclude that $A$ is not diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070655/assets/c8716d15efb054f488535b139e201dda7bc61a3fb8880caf50d65bee515c2cc1.png)" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
